=======

LangGraph Syntax Comparison
Normal Edge
graph.add_edge("planner", "researcher")

Always goes to researcher.

Conditional Edge
graph.add_conditional_edges(
    "planner",
    router,
    {
        "researcher": "researcher",
        "writer": "writer",
        "coder": "coder",
    }
)

The router function decides whether execution goes to researcher, writer, or coder.

===========

| Feature              | Normal Edge          | Conditional Edge                                |
| -------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Decision making      | ❌ No                 | ✅ Yes                                           |
| Number of next nodes | One                  | Multiple                                        |
| Execution            | Fixed                | Dynamic                                         |
| Method               | `add_edge()`         | `add_conditional_edges()`                       |
| Used for             | Sequential workflows | Agent routing, branching, decision-making       |
| Example              | Planner → Writer     | Planner → Writer **or** Researcher **or** Coder |


=======

In Agentic AI and Multi-Agent systems, conditional edges are one of the most important features. 
They allow a planner or router agent to dynamically select the appropriate specialized agent based on the user's request or the current workflow state, 
making the system intelligent rather than strictly sequential.

